@@ -3,3 +3,38 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Running the tests
+
+`npm test` runs the test suite with Node's built-in test runner. The sources are TypeScript that Node
+runs directly by stripping the types (Node 22.18 or later), so modules import each other with the `.ts`
+extension; `tsconfig.json` rewrites those to `.js` when compiling to `dist`.
+
+## JSON Pointer (RFC 6901)
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const config = { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }, { host: "b.example" }], "a/b": true };
+
+resolvePointer(config, "/servers/1/host"); // "b.example"
+resolvePointer(config, "/a~1b");           // true: "~1" stands for "/" and "~0" for "~"
+resolvePointer(config, "");                // the whole document
+```
+
+- `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` returns the value the pointer refers to: the value itself, not a
+  copy.
+- It throws an `InvalidPointerError` when the pointer is not valid JSON Pointer syntax: it is not
+  empty and does not start with `/`, or it contains a `~` that is not followed by `0` or `1`.
+- It throws an `UnresolvablePointerError` when the pointer does not refer to a value of the document:
+  a missing member, an array index that is out of range or not written as `0` or digits without a
+  leading zero, the array token `-` (it names the element after the last one, which does not exist),
+  or a token applied to a string, number, boolean or null. The error's `token` property names the
+  reference token that failed.
+- Both errors extend `JsonPointerError`, which carries the `pointer`.
+- `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the unescaped reference tokens, for code that needs to walk or build
+  on a pointer itself.
+
+Only the JSON string form of a pointer is accepted. A pointer taken from a URI fragment (`#/foo`) must
+be percent-decoded and stripped of its `#` first. See
+[ADR 0001](docs/adr/0001-json-pointer-resolution.md) for the choices made where the RFC leaves room.
