+# ADR 0001: JSON Pointer resolution
+
+- Version: 1
+- Date: 2026-10-21
+- Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer` implements RFC 6901 and is the base for later features, such as JSON Patch
+(RFC 6902), that address values by pointer. RFC 6901 leaves the error handling to the application
+(section 7) and leaves some cases open.
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **Two error classes under a common base.** `InvalidPointerError` (bad syntax) and
+   `UnresolvablePointerError` (valid pointer, no such value) both extend `JsonPointerError`. Callers
+   such as JSON Patch must tell a malformed request from a missing target, and can catch both at once.
+2. **`-` never resolves.** The RFC says it names the nonexistent element after the last one. Resolving
+   it is an error; features that append to arrays handle `-` themselves on top of `parsePointer`.
+3. **Strict array indices.** Only `0` or digits without a leading zero index an array. `01`, `-1`,
+   `1.0`, `+1` or `length` are not indices, so they do not resolve against an array. On an object the
+   same tokens are ordinary member names.
+4. **Own members only.** Members are looked up with `Object.hasOwn`, so a pointer such as
+   `/constructor` or `/__proto__` cannot reach the JavaScript prototype chain. Documents come from
+   outside our services, and inherited properties are not part of the JSON value.
+5. **No Unicode normalization.** Member names are compared as they are, as the RFC requires.
+6. **JSON string representation only.** The URI fragment form (section 6) is not decoded; a caller
+   that has one percent-decodes it first. Keeping one form avoids guessing which one a string is in.
+7. **The value itself is returned, not a copy**, so that later features can update it in place.
